# 16. Cross-Case Findings

## Finding 1 — Dedicated funding produces continuity.

Pittman-Robertson and Dingell-Johnson demonstrate the durability of dedicated revenue mechanisms.

## Finding 2 — Dedicated funding does not guarantee optimal allocation.

Stable formulas can remain only partially connected to changing ecological conditions.

## Finding 3 — Matching requirements can substantially increase total investment.

NAWCA provides a clear example of federal funding being accompanied by significant partner contributions.

## Finding 4 — Leverage depends on recipient capacity.

A matching requirement has different effects on a large conservation organization than on a small or resource-constrained organization.

## Finding 5 — Competitive funding can target ecological priorities.

State Wildlife Grants and NAWCA demonstrate how competitive mechanisms can direct resources toward specified conservation objectives.

## Finding 6 — Competitive funding also creates administrative selection effects.

Organizations capable of preparing sophisticated applications may be better positioned to compete.

## Finding 7 — Funding mechanisms can deliberately change institutional behavior.

CFLRP, for example, links funding with collaboration, landscape-scale planning, implementation, and monitoring.

## Finding 8 — Private-land funding is fundamentally different from public-land funding.

The government cannot simply order implementation on private property through a grant program.

Participation and stewardship become additional steps in the causal chain.

## Finding 9 — Scientific funding produces decision value.

Fisheries assessment demonstrates that some conservation investments matter primarily because they improve subsequent decisions.

## Finding 10 — Prevention is difficult to measure.

A successful preventive program may produce an avoided ecological decline rather than a visible restoration event.

## Finding 11 — One-time funding is poorly suited to permanent responsibilities.

Settlement and damages funding can be highly valuable for specific restoration but cannot reliably substitute for institutional baseline funding.

## Finding 12 — Maintenance remains a major funding gap.

Initial project funding does not guarantee resources for long-term stewardship.

## Finding 13 — Funding fragmentation becomes more consequential at ecological scales.

Watersheds, landscapes, migratory species, and large restoration systems frequently require multiple funding streams.

## Finding 14 — Funding and institutional capacity are inseparable.

The value of a financial allocation depends partly on whether recipients can use it effectively.

