# 17. Funding Failure Typology

The case studies allow the earlier funding-failure framework to be refined.

### 1. Insufficient Funding

The available resources cannot reasonably support the required responsibility.

### 2. Unstable Funding

Funding fluctuates too much for the ecological timescale.

### 3. Misaligned Funding

Resources are distributed without adequate correspondence to ecological need.

### 4. Inflexible Funding

Funds cannot be redirected as conditions change.

### 5. Administrative Friction

Application, reporting, procurement, or grant requirements consume excessive capacity.

### 6. Capacity-Mismatch Funding

Recipients receive resources they lack the organizational ability to deploy.

### 7. Fragmented Funding

Multiple programs address pieces of a connected ecological problem without adequate coordination.

### 8. Maintenance Failure

Funding ends before ecological benefits become self-sustaining.

### 9. Measurement Failure

Funding produces activities without sufficient monitoring to determine ecological effect.

### 10. Attribution Failure

Ecological outcomes cannot reasonably be connected to the investment.

